**Rebuttal:**

We thank Reviewer SCWX for the detailed and helpful feedback. We respond to the concerns as follows:

**Reg. novelty beyond pipeline composition**

We agree that the individual modules themselves are not new in isolation. Our contribution lies in a task-specific formulation for game short-form generation. In particular, we detect gameplay events from on-screen visual cues, group temporally related events into interaction-level segments, and generate captions from those segments rather than from isolated events.

Thus, our contribution is not a new detector, clustering method, or decoder by itself, but the way these components are combined into a unified framework for gameplay highlights. To verify that the gain does not come merely from assembling standard modules, we compare event-level and interaction-level settings under the same encoder-decoder configuration.

| Setting | BLEU-3 | BLEU-4 | METEOR | ROUGE-L | CIDEr |
|:--------|:------:|:------:|:------:|:-------:|:-----:|
| Event-level | 2.59 | 1.12 | 14.74 | 13.57 | 5.64 |
| Interaction-level | 3.17 | 1.54 | 16.05 | 15.31 | 7.14 |

The improvements across all metrics suggest that the gain is not merely due to assembling existing components, but arises from the interaction-level formulation. We hope this helps clarify the key novelty of our work.

**Reg. evaluation of the proposed task**

We agree that, because we formulate a new end-to-end task, its performance should be demonstrated at the level of the final short-form output rather than only through intermediate analyses. In our pipeline, caption generation is not performed on externally given segments; it is conditioned on the highlights selected by the proposed interaction module. The reported captioning results therefore already reflect the quality of the preceding highlight selection stage.

This coupling is also supported by the comparison between the event-level and interaction-level settings above. Changing the highlight unit leads to consistent improvements in downstream captioning results under the same encoder-decoder configuration.

We further evaluate performance on the proposed task through a user study on real user-recorded gameplay videos (see Table 8). Participants judge the generated short-form videos as complete outputs, including both highlight selection and caption generation. The demo used in the study is provided here: [LINK]. We will clarify this evaluation protocol more explicitly in the revision.

**Reg. broader baselines**

We appreciate the suggestion and expanded the comparisons at both stages of the pipeline.

For **highlight selection**, we compare our KDE-based extraction against DBSCAN and Sliding Window using manually aligned ground-truth highlight intervals from the same matches. Qualitative comparisons are also provided here: https://anonymous.4open.science/r/anonymous-submission-27793/mm.md.

| Method | Precision | Recall | F1 | Mean # Segments |
|:------:|:---------:|:------:|:--:|:---------------:|
| DBSCAN | 1.0000 | 0.0476 | 0.0903 | 2.33 |
| Sliding Window | 0.9848 | 0.1504 | 0.2565 | 4.67 |
| KDE h=3.2 | 0.9480 | 0.2123 | 0.3433 | 6.00 |

DBSCAN is overly sparse, while Sliding Window improves coverage but remains less adaptive. KDE achieves the best recall and F1 while preserving high precision, indicating a better balance for salient interaction segments. The same pattern is also observed in the qualitative comparisons (Figs. 1–3).

For **caption generation**, the only reported baseline in the original submission was the game-specific model LoL-V2T. We therefore added a zero-shot general-purpose video-language model (Video-LLaVA) and more general dense video captioning baselines (Vid2Seq and CM²).

| Model | BLEU-3 | BLEU-4 | METEOR | ROUGE-L | CIDEr | CLIP-S | BERT-S |
|:------|:------:|:------:|:------:|:-------:|:-----:|:------:|:------:|
| Video-LLaVA | 0.13 | 0.00 | 3.14 | 6.84 | 0.01 | 26.49 | 7.89 |
| Vid2Seq | 0.01 | 0.01 | 3.18 | 2.13 | 0.01 | 21.99 | 18.70 |
| CM² | 0.09 | 0.00 | 8.33 | 10.56 | 0.01 | 24.08 | 14.75 |
| LoL-V2T | 2.17 | 1.25 | 12.26 | 12.66 | 8.34 | 28.95 | 17.86 |
| Ours | 3.17 | 1.54 | 16.05 | 15.31 | 7.14 | 28.06 | 24.64 |

Video-LLaVA is evaluated zero-shot, while Vid2Seq, CM², LoL-V2T, and our method are trained on the same dataset.

These expanded comparisons show that the proposed pipeline is competitive at both stages: 
- KDE provides stronger highlight selection than other segment extraction methods. 
- The resulting interaction-level captions outperform broader captioning baselines on most metrics. 

We will make these expanded comparisons more explicit in the revision.
